<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Suceava</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Suceava is a city in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, in the historical region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukovina">Bukovina</a>. It was the capital of the Principality of Moldavia from about 1388 until 1564, when the court moved to Iași, and its fortress withstood an Ottoman siege under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> in 1476. It belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> from 1775 to 1918 and is surrounded by the painted monasteries of Bukovina.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suceava.jpg" alt="Suceava" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rosenborg BK Fan · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palatul_Administrativ_din_Suceava_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
